<article class="env-definition" id="7562DB">
  <p><a class="environment-identifier" href="/tag/7562DB">Definition <span data-tag="7562DB">2.4.2.1</span>.</a>Cartesian fibrationfibration!Cartesian We will say that a map $p: X \rightarrow S$ of simplicial sets is a <em>Cartesian fibration</em> if the following conditions are satisfied: </p>
  <p><ul>
  <li class="custom-item" data-marker="$(1)$"><p>
The map $p$ is an inner fibration. 
</p>
</li><li class="custom-item" data-marker="$(2)$"><p>
For every edge $f: x \rightarrow y$ of $S$ and every vertex $\widetilde{y}$ of $X$ with $p(\widetilde{y}) = y$, there exists a $p$-Cartesian edge $\widetilde{f}: \widetilde{x} \rightarrow \widetilde{y}$ with $p(\widetilde{f}) = f$. 
</p>
</li>
</ul></p>
  <p>We say that $p$ is a <em>coCartesian fibration</em> if the opposite map $p^{op}: X^{op} \rightarrow S^{op}$ is a Cartesian fibration.fibration!coCartesiancoCartesian fibration </p>
